    "title": "Chapter 9 — Two Side Hustles the Source Would Now Avoid",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Chapter 9 — Two Side Hustles the Source Would Now Avoid\nThe source revisits two options from an earlier list and says it would no longer recommend them in the same way. The point is not that nobody can succeed with them; it is that the conditions have changed.",
      "1. Repetitive, templated faceless short-form channels\nThe source distinguishes original faceless content from channels that repeatedly publish near-identical videos using the same template.",
      "It says platform policies had become more restrictive toward repetitive or inauthentic content and warns that channels using mass-produced templates may lose monetisation.",
      "The practical lesson:\n- Faceless is a format, not a business model.\n- Original research and storytelling still matter.\n- Repetitive low-effort content is vulnerable to platform policy changes.\n- Monetisation eligibility should never be assumed.\nIf you choose to make faceless content, create original scripts, meaningful editing, real commentary, and distinct value. Follow the current platform monetisation policies rather than relying on old advice.",
      "2. Beginner online writing as a standalone service\nThe source says it would be more cautious about recommending generic beginner writing work because AI has changed demand for basic writing services. It references a study that reported a decline in writing jobs on a major freelance platform after the introduction of ChatGPT.",
      "That is a source-reported finding, not a complete picture of the writing market. Writing work still exists, but generic, undifferentiated output faces stronger competition.",
      "A more defensible direction is to combine writing with something harder to commoditise:\n- Subject-matter expertise.\n- Original research.\n- Interviews.\n- Technical understanding.\n- Editorial judgement.\n- Distribution strategy.\n- Conversion-focused experimentation.\n- Industry-specific knowledge.\nThe lesson is not “never write.” It is “do not sell generic text as though it were a differentiated service.”"
    ]
