      "Chapter 11 — Build a Side Hustle Around Your Academic Schedule",
      "Protect the main priority\nA student side hustle should fit around education, not quietly consume every available hour.",
      "Start with a realistic weekly capacity. If you have exams, projects, or a demanding semester, reduce the workload rather than pretending you can maintain the same schedule year-round.",
      "A sample 8-hour weekly plan\nThis is an example schedule, not a required workload.\n| Activity | Hours per week |\n| --- | --- |\n| Core side-hustle work | 4 |\n| Learning and improvement | 1 |\n| Outreach or distribution | 1 |\n| Customer or audience feedback | 1 |\n| Review and planning | 1 |\n| Total | 8 |",
      "Some models require different allocations. UGC may need more production time; newsletters need research and writing; AI evaluation may involve fixed task windows.",
      "Use a semester-aware schedule",
      "Normal teaching weeks\n- Maintain a small, repeatable workload.\n- Publish or deliver on a predictable schedule.\n- Keep one day or time block free for coursework.",
      "Assignment-heavy weeks\n- Reduce content production.\n- Pause experiments that are not urgent.\n- Complete existing customer commitments first.",
      "Exam periods\n- Avoid launching a complicated new project.\n- Maintain only essential operations.\n- Do not accept work you cannot deliver.",
      "Semester breaks\n- Run a focused growth experiment.\n- Build a portfolio or product.\n- Improve systems and documentation.",
      "The weekly review\nAsk:\n- What did I actually complete?\n- Did I make progress toward a customer, user, or paid task?\n- Which activity created the most useful evidence?\n- What consumed time without producing learning?\n- Is the workload harming my studies?\n- What is the single most important action next week?"
